# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites
# every checked-out text file to CRLF.  That is harmless for the C++ sources and
# the build script, but `tools/greencurve-setup.sh` is copied verbatim into the
# Linux release archive: a CRLF checkout shipped `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which
# Linux resolves as an interpreter literally named `bash\r`
# ("env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory"), and every heredoc body -- the
# installed .desktop entry among them -- carried a trailing CR.  The archive was
# therefore only usable when it happened to be packaged on a Linux host.
#
# Pin every shell script to LF in the working tree regardless of host or
# autocrlf setting.  build.py additionally rewrites Linux release text to LF at
# packaging time, so the archive is correct even from a working tree that
# predates this file.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Linux release archive is a .tar.xz; the Windows one is a .7z.  Neither is
# text, and neither is ever tracked, but say so explicitly so no future
# `text=auto` rule can be tempted to touch them.
*.7z binary
*.tar.xz binary

# Fuzz seeds are exact byte strings, not text.  Most existing corpora contain
# NUL bytes and so are auto-detected as binary, but the update_manifest seeds
# are printable ASCII -- on a Windows checkout `core.autocrlf` would rewrite
# their line endings, silently changing what the corpus covers.  One of those
# seeds exists specifically to carry CRLF, so the normalization would collapse
# it into a duplicate of the LF seed beside it.
tests/fuzz-corpus/** binary
